1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,979
This photograph is called the most terrifying picture ever taken in space.

2
00:00:04,979 --> 00:00:11,174
A man, completely alone, a hundred meters from his ship, with nothing but blackness behind him.

3
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:19,584
His name was Bruce McCandless. And here is the strange part. Nothing went wrong that day.

4
00:00:19,584 --> 00:00:21,861
He flew back. He lived.

5
00:00:22,999 --> 00:00:26,595
But the photographs you are about to see are different.

6
00:00:26,595 --> 00:00:29,112
Behind each one of them, somebody died.

7
00:00:30,233 --> 00:00:34,529
Twenty one astronauts and cosmonauts have been killed flying spaceflight missions.

8
00:00:34,529 --> 00:00:38,435
Hundreds more died on the ground, getting the machines ready.

9
00:00:39,566 --> 00:00:42,082
Some burned. Some froze in the dark.

10
00:00:42,082 --> 00:00:48,552
Three of them died in space itself, and their bodies came home without a single mark on them.

11
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:56,447
For decades, some of these deaths were state secrets. Faces were erased from photographs.

12
00:00:56,447 --> 00:00:59,339
Files stayed sealed for thirty years.

13
00:01:00,466 --> 00:01:02,621
This is the complete story.

14
00:01:02,621 --> 00:01:06,931
Every disaster, every cover up, every warning that was ignored.

15
00:01:06,931 --> 00:01:09,948
Told through the real photographs that survived.

16
00:01:11,066 --> 00:01:15,942
By the end, you will understand why astronauts call their job riding the bullet.

17
00:01:15,942 --> 00:01:18,380
And why they keep climbing in anyway.

18
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,191
Chapter one. The first passenger.

19
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:27,188
Before space killed a single human being, it claimed its first passenger.

20
00:01:27,188 --> 00:01:29,759
And she never had a choice about going.

21
00:01:30,899 --> 00:01:37,662
Moscow, 1957. A three year old stray dog is picked off the streets.

22
00:01:37,662 --> 00:01:42,864
Small, calm, even tempered. The scientists name her Laika. Barker.

23
00:01:43,999 --> 00:01:49,266
Sputnik 1 has just shocked the world, and Khrushchev demands a follow-up spectacular within a month.

24
00:01:49,266 --> 00:01:53,873
A month is not enough time to design a capsule that can come back.

25
00:01:54,999 --> 00:01:59,916
Everyone involved knows what that means. Sputnik 2 will be a one way trip.

26
00:01:59,916 --> 00:02:04,481
Laika is sent to orbit with no plan, and no possibility, of return.

27
00:02:05,599 --> 00:02:10,456
For decades, the official story said she survived nearly a week in orbit.

28
00:02:10,456 --> 00:02:13,070
The truth came out only in 2002.

29
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:15,849
A thermal control failure.

30
00:02:15,849 --> 00:02:21,622
The cabin overheated, and Laika died within hours of launch, on her fourth orbit.

31
00:02:21,622 --> 00:02:25,745
Frightened, and alone, in a machine she could not understand.

32
00:02:26,866 --> 00:02:30,907
One of the scientists who trained her, Oleg Gazenko, said decades later.

33
00:02:30,907 --> 00:02:35,957
We did not learn enough from the mission to justify the death of the dog.

34
00:02:37,099 --> 00:02:44,397
Remember the shape of this story. A deadline set by politics. A known, fatal risk.

35
00:02:44,397 --> 00:02:51,695
And the truth, hidden for decades. You will see this shape again and again tonight.

36
00:02:51,695 --> 00:02:52,668
With people.

37
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:56,646
Chapter two. The erased cosmonaut.

38
00:02:57,766 --> 00:02:58,493
March 1961.

39
00:02:58,493 --> 00:03:04,312
The Soviet Union is three weeks away from making Yuri Gagarin the first human in space.

40
00:03:04,312 --> 00:03:06,857
The whole program is a state secret.

41
00:03:07,999 --> 00:03:14,316
In a Moscow facility, twenty cosmonaut candidates are being tested to destruction.

42
00:03:14,316 --> 00:03:18,526
Centrifuges. Isolation. Days alone in sealed steel chambers.

43
00:03:19,666 --> 00:03:22,085
The youngest of them is Valentin Bondarenko.

44
00:03:22,085 --> 00:03:28,652
He is twenty four years old, a fighter pilot, and by every account the kindest man in the group.

45
00:03:29,799 --> 00:03:34,462
On March 23rd he is finishing day ten inside an isolation chamber.

46
00:03:34,462 --> 00:03:39,125
A small sealed room, pumped full of pure oxygen at low pressure.

47
00:03:40,266 --> 00:03:43,209
Pure oxygen changes the rules of fire.

48
00:03:43,209 --> 00:03:49,095
Things that will not burn in normal air ignite instantly. Flames spread like liquid.

49
00:03:50,233 --> 00:03:55,737
Bondarenko removes his medical sensors and wipes his skin with an alcohol soaked cotton pad.

50
00:03:55,737 --> 00:03:57,939
Then he tosses the pad aside.

51
00:03:59,066 --> 00:04:05,387
It lands on the coil of a hot plate. In pure oxygen, the flash is instantaneous.

52
00:04:05,387 --> 00:04:08,548
Within seconds the entire chamber is on fire.

53
00:04:09,666 --> 00:04:14,302
The chamber cannot be opened quickly. The pressure difference seals the door shut.

54
00:04:14,302 --> 00:04:17,868
It takes almost half an hour to get him out.

55
00:04:18,999 --> 00:04:24,834
When they finally do, Bondarenko is still conscious. He keeps whispering the same words.

56
00:04:24,834 --> 00:04:28,168
It was my fault. It was my fault.

57
00:04:29,299 --> 00:04:35,270
Doctors fight for him for hours, but he has burns over almost his entire body.

58
00:04:35,270 --> 00:04:37,658
Valentin Bondarenko dies sixteen hours later.

59
00:04:38,799 --> 00:04:45,133
And then, he disappears. The Soviet Union simply erases him. His death is classified.

60
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:48,752
His face is airbrushed out of group photographs.

61
00:04:49,899 --> 00:04:54,353
For twenty five years, the West does not know he existed.

62
00:04:54,353 --> 00:04:59,616
The truth is only published in 1986, during the Soviet policy of openness.

63
00:05:00,766 --> 00:05:07,401
Here is what makes this more than a tragedy. A sealed chamber. Pure oxygen. A spark.

64
00:05:07,401 --> 00:05:10,719
Rescuers who cannot open the door in time.

65
00:05:11,866 --> 00:05:18,102
Six years later, the exact same fire will happen again. Not in Moscow. In Florida.

66
00:05:18,102 --> 00:05:21,427
And it will kill three of America's finest.

67
00:05:22,566 --> 00:05:28,191
Some historians believe that if Bondarenko's death had been public, NASA might have seen the danger.

68
00:05:28,191 --> 00:05:31,004
The secret may have cost three more lives.

69
00:05:32,133 --> 00:05:38,089
But before we get to that fire, we need to talk about the machine that killed more astronauts than any rocket ever did.

70
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:42,002
Chapter three. Death before launch.

71
00:05:43,133 --> 00:05:47,198
This is the T-38 Talon. A supersonic training jet.

72
00:05:47,198 --> 00:05:53,974
Every NASA astronaut of the 1960s flew one, to keep their piloting skills razor sharp.

73
00:05:55,099 --> 00:05:57,600
It is small, fast, and unforgiving.

74
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:04,268
And in the space of sixteen months, it killed four astronauts before they ever reached space.

75
00:06:05,399 --> 00:06:06,246
October 1964.

76
00:06:06,246 --> 00:06:13,445
Theodore Freeman, one of the newest astronauts, is on approach to Ellington Air Force Base near Houston.

77
00:06:14,566 --> 00:06:17,300
A snow goose smashes through his canopy.

78
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:22,377
Fragments of shattered plexiglass are sucked into both engines, and they flame out.

79
00:06:23,499 --> 00:06:24,952
Freeman does everything right.

80
00:06:24,952 --> 00:06:31,127
He steers the dying jet away from the buildings below, and ejects at the last possible moment.

81
00:06:32,266 --> 00:06:38,533
It is too late. He is too low. His parachute never fully opens.

82
00:06:38,533 --> 00:06:42,872
Theodore Freeman becomes the first American astronaut to die.

83
00:06:43,999 --> 00:06:46,010
Sixteen months later, February 1966.

84
00:06:46,010 --> 00:06:53,246
Elliot See and Charles Bassett are the prime crew of Gemini 9, flying to Saint Louis for training.

85
00:06:54,366 --> 00:07:00,333
Their destination is the McDonnell plant, where their own spacecraft is being built.

86
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:04,005
The weather is terrible. Rain, fog, low cloud.

87
00:07:05,133 --> 00:07:10,203
See comes in low, loses sight of the runway, and circles for another attempt.

88
00:07:10,203 --> 00:07:13,100
His wingtip clips the roof of a building.

89
00:07:14,233 --> 00:07:16,599
Not just any building. Building 101.

90
00:07:16,599 --> 00:07:22,514
The jet crashes into the very factory where the Gemini 9 capsule is being assembled.

91
00:07:23,633 --> 00:07:29,667
See and Bassett die instantly, a few hundred feet from the spacecraft that was supposed to carry them into orbit.

92
00:07:30,799 --> 00:07:36,658
Inside the building, workers are injured by falling debris, but the capsule itself is untouched.

93
00:07:36,658 --> 00:07:39,001
The backup crew flies the mission.

94
00:07:40,133 --> 00:07:46,306
Four dead astronauts, and not one of them ever left the atmosphere. The lesson was brutal.

95
00:07:46,306 --> 00:07:50,164
The most dangerous part of spaceflight is everything around it.

96
00:07:51,299 --> 00:07:55,906
NASA absorbed the losses and kept moving. The Moon deadline was everything.

97
00:07:55,906 --> 00:08:00,128
But the corners being cut were about to catch fire. Literally.

98
00:08:01,266 --> 00:08:04,583
Chapter four. The fire on pad 34.

99
00:08:05,733 --> 00:08:08,724
January 27, 1967. Cape Kennedy, Florida.

100
00:08:08,724 --> 00:08:14,706
Three men climb into the first Apollo spacecraft for a routine test.

101
00:08:14,706 --> 00:08:17,697
The rocket is not even fueled.

102
00:08:18,833 --> 00:08:25,122
Gus Grissom, one of the original Mercury astronauts, and a favorite to be first on the Moon.

103
00:08:25,122 --> 00:08:30,301
Ed White, the first American to walk in space. And Roger Chaffee, the rookie.

104
00:08:31,433 --> 00:08:34,618
The test is called a plugs out test.

105
00:08:34,618 --> 00:08:39,793
The spacecraft runs on internal power, sealed, as if it were really flying.

106
00:08:39,793 --> 00:08:41,386
Nobody considers it dangerous.

107
00:08:42,533 --> 00:08:49,631
But the capsule is a mess. Miles of wiring, much of it poorly insulated.

108
00:08:49,631 --> 00:08:53,687
Flammable nylon netting everywhere. Velcro on every wall.

109
00:08:54,833 --> 00:09:00,012
And it is filled with pure oxygen, at higher than sea level pressure.

110
00:09:00,012 --> 00:09:04,394
The same silent killer that took Bondarenko, waiting quietly in Florida.

111
00:09:05,533 --> 00:09:08,033
Grissom knows the spacecraft has problems.

112
00:09:08,033 --> 00:09:14,284
He has already hung a lemon on the simulator in protest. The communications keep failing.

113
00:09:15,433 --> 00:09:18,446
His last recorded complaint, minutes before the end, is chilling.

114
00:09:18,446 --> 00:09:23,871
How are we going to get to the Moon if we can't talk between two or three buildings?

115
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,760
At 6:31 PM, somewhere under Grissom's seat, a damaged wire arcs.

116
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:34,952
In that atmosphere, it is like striking a match inside a bomb.

117
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:41,363
The first words from the capsule are calm. Fire. I smell fire.

118
00:09:41,363 --> 00:09:46,627
Two seconds later, Chaffee's voice. We've got a fire in the cockpit.

119
00:09:47,766 --> 00:09:52,411
In pure oxygen the fire does not spread. It detonates.

120
00:09:52,411 --> 00:09:58,450
Within seconds the temperature soars past a thousand degrees. The hull splits open.

121
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:03,806
The last transmission is a scream, cut off after eighteen seconds.

122
00:10:03,806 --> 00:10:09,160
From first spark to silence, the entire fire lasts less than half a minute.

123
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:12,397
The hatch is the final cruelty.

124
00:10:12,397 --> 00:10:18,342
It opens inward, held shut by the cabin pressure, and needs at least ninety seconds to open.

125
00:10:18,342 --> 00:10:19,391
They had none.

126
00:10:20,533 --> 00:10:25,896
Pad workers fight through smoke and heat to reach them, some fearing the whole rocket will explode.

127
00:10:25,896 --> 00:10:28,735
It takes five minutes to get the hatch open.

128
00:10:29,866 --> 00:10:33,349
Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger Chaffee are gone.

129
00:10:33,349 --> 00:10:40,315
The autopsy finds they died of asphyxiation from toxic smoke, in seconds, before the worst of the burns.

130
00:10:41,433 --> 00:10:42,608
America is stunned.

131
00:10:42,608 --> 00:10:50,837
Three astronauts, dead on the ground, in a test nobody thought was dangerous, in a rocket that never left the pad.

132
00:10:51,966 --> 00:10:53,683
The investigation is merciless.

133
00:10:53,683 --> 00:11:01,840
It finds shoddy workmanship, ignored hazards, and a culture that had normalized risk in the race to the Moon.

134
00:11:02,966 --> 00:11:08,792
Flight director Gene Kranz gathers his team and delivers a speech that still defines NASA.

135
00:11:08,792 --> 00:11:14,617
From this day forward, mission control will be known by two words. Tough and competent.

136
00:11:15,766 --> 00:11:21,793
The Apollo spacecraft is rebuilt from scratch. A hatch that opens outward in seconds.

137
00:11:21,793 --> 00:11:26,528
Fireproof materials. Mixed gas on the pad instead of pure oxygen.

138
00:11:27,666 --> 00:11:30,166
Many at NASA later said the same thing.

139
00:11:30,166 --> 00:11:33,290
Without the fire, we would never have reached the Moon.

140
00:11:33,290 --> 00:11:35,790
The lessons were written in those three lives.

141
00:11:36,933 --> 00:11:42,849
Pad 34 still stands at Cape Canaveral, abandoned. On its base, a plaque reads.

142
00:11:42,849 --> 00:11:48,342
They gave their lives in service to their country. Ad astra per aspera.

143
00:11:48,342 --> 00:11:51,300
A rough road leads to the stars.

144
00:11:52,433 --> 00:11:59,864
Exactly three months after the fire, on the other side of the world, the Soviet Union was about to make an even darker choice.

145
00:11:59,864 --> 00:12:02,960
Launch a man in a ship they knew was broken.

146
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:07,653
Chapter five. The man who fell from space.

147
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:09,683
April 1967.

148
00:12:09,683 --> 00:12:16,312
The Soviet leadership wants a spectacular space triumph for the fiftieth anniversary of the revolution.

149
00:12:16,312 --> 00:12:19,406
The new Soyuz spacecraft will deliver it.

150
00:12:20,533 --> 00:12:24,265
There is one problem. The Soyuz is not ready.

151
00:12:24,265 --> 00:12:27,583
Its three unmanned test flights have all failed.

152
00:12:27,583 --> 00:12:30,486
Engineers have logged hundreds of design faults.

153
00:12:31,633 --> 00:12:34,842
The man assigned to fly it is Vladimir Komarov.

154
00:12:34,842 --> 00:12:39,835
Veteran cosmonaut, brilliant engineer, and one of the most respected men in the program.

155
00:12:40,966 --> 00:12:47,079
His backup is his best friend. The most famous man on Earth. Yuri Gagarin.

156
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:53,318
By some accounts, Komarov told friends he knew the flight was likely to kill him.

157
00:12:53,318 --> 00:12:59,119
Asked why he did not refuse, his answer was simple. If I don't fly, they'll send Yura.

158
00:13:00,266 --> 00:13:04,980
Whether or not every detail of that story is true, this much is documented.

159
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:09,357
The engineers knew the ship had faults, and the launch went ahead anyway.

160
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:15,248
April 23rd. Soyuz 1 lifts off with Komarov alone on board.

161
00:13:15,248 --> 00:13:18,702
The problems begin within minutes of reaching orbit.

162
00:13:19,833 --> 00:13:23,169
One of the two solar panels fails to unfold.

163
00:13:23,169 --> 00:13:26,877
The ship is starved of power, dragging a dead wing.

164
00:13:26,877 --> 00:13:29,472
The navigation sensors fail one by one.

165
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:37,047
For a day, Komarov wrestles the crippled ship, manually orienting it while spinning between day and night.

166
00:13:37,047 --> 00:13:40,082
Mission control decides to bring him home early.

167
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:47,499
Getting home requires a precise engine burn, then a descent through the atmosphere, then parachutes.

168
00:13:47,499 --> 00:13:52,119
Komarov, flying half blind, nails the burn on his second attempt.

169
00:13:53,266 --> 00:13:58,803
He survives reentry. The capsule streaks over the steppe, slowing, glowing.

170
00:13:58,803 --> 00:14:02,830
At seven kilometers up, the drogue parachute deploys.

171
00:14:02,830 --> 00:14:06,353
And then, the failure that kills him.

172
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:10,352
The main parachute never leaves its container.

173
00:14:10,352 --> 00:14:16,057
A pressure sensor fault, and a container that was likely deformed and rough inside.

174
00:14:16,057 --> 00:14:17,688
The chute is stuck.

175
00:14:18,833 --> 00:14:20,769
Komarov triggers the reserve chute.

176
00:14:20,769 --> 00:14:25,417
It tangles instantly in the still spinning drogue of the failed main.

177
00:14:25,417 --> 00:14:27,741
Nothing is slowing the capsule now.

178
00:14:28,866 --> 00:14:34,141
Soyuz 1 hits the ground at roughly one hundred and forty kilometers per hour.

179
00:14:34,141 --> 00:14:38,662
The impact flattens the capsule. Then the retro rockets ignite the wreck.

180
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:43,979
Recovery crews arrive to find burning metal half buried in the soil.

181
00:14:43,979 --> 00:14:48,159
Vladimir Komarov becomes the first human being to die on a spaceflight.

182
00:14:49,300 --> 00:14:54,448
All that could be recovered was buried in the Kremlin wall with full honors.

183
00:14:54,448 --> 00:14:58,861
Gagarin was reportedly furious, convinced the political pressure had killed his friend.

184
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:06,019
Gagarin himself would be dead within a year, killed in a jet crash still argued about today.

185
00:15:06,019 --> 00:15:09,561
The program lost its two brightest stars back to back.

186
00:15:10,700 --> 00:15:14,740
The Soyuz was grounded for eighteen months and redesigned.

187
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:19,678
Today, its descendants are among the most reliable spacecraft ever built.

188
00:15:19,678 --> 00:15:22,821
That reliability was bought at Komarov's price.

189
00:15:23,966 --> 00:15:28,366
And the friend Komarov flew to protect. The first human in space.

190
00:15:28,366 --> 00:15:34,232
The most protected man in the Soviet Union. He had less than a year left himself.

191
00:15:35,366 --> 00:15:39,154
Chapter six. The death of the first man in space.

192
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:46,769
After Komarov's death, Yuri Gagarin was a national treasure the state could not afford to lose.

193
00:15:46,769 --> 00:15:50,409
He was banned from spaceflight. Wrapped in cotton wool.

194
00:15:51,533 --> 00:15:58,910
But Gagarin was a fighter pilot before he was a symbol, and he fought his way back to the one thing they would still allow.

195
00:15:58,910 --> 00:15:59,500
Flying jets.

196
00:16:00,633 --> 00:16:01,974
March 27, 1968.

197
00:16:01,974 --> 00:16:08,677
Gagarin takes off in a MiG-15 trainer with instructor Vladimir Seryogin for a routine flight.

198
00:16:08,677 --> 00:16:13,146
The weather is poor. Cloud layers stacked to the ground.

199
00:16:14,266 --> 00:16:20,161
Minutes after entering the exercise zone, Gagarin radios that he is finished and returning to base.

200
00:16:20,161 --> 00:16:24,950
His voice is calm. It is the last anyone ever hears from him.

201
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:31,154
The MiG dives out of the clouds at extreme speed and hits the forest floor.

202
00:16:31,154 --> 00:16:36,209
Both men are killed instantly. The first man in space is dead at thirty four.

203
00:16:37,333 --> 00:16:42,860
The Soviet Union goes into shock. And then it does the thing it always does.

204
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:44,334
It classifies the investigation.

205
00:16:45,466 --> 00:16:51,244
Into that silence pour fifty years of theories. He was drunk. He was murdered.

206
00:16:51,244 --> 00:16:58,261
He faked his death. He was punished for falling out of favor. None of it has evidence.

207
00:16:58,261 --> 00:17:01,975
All of it filled the vacuum the secrecy created.

208
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:11,193
The most credible account came out in 2013, from Alexei Leonov, who had access to the declassified file.

209
00:17:11,193 --> 00:17:17,938
Another aircraft, a supersonic Su-15, was flying in the area, far below its permitted altitude.

210
00:17:19,066 --> 00:17:28,026
Leonov believed it passed within meters of Gagarin's MiG at supersonic speed, and its wake flipped the trainer into an unrecoverable spin in the clouds.

211
00:17:28,026 --> 00:17:30,534
The other pilot was never publicly named.

212
00:17:31,666 --> 00:17:37,100
Whatever the final truth, the pattern held even for the most famous man in the country.

213
00:17:37,100 --> 00:17:40,835
In the Soviet space program, even Gagarin's death was a secret.

214
00:17:41,966 --> 00:17:45,862
His ashes were placed in the Kremlin wall, beside his friend Komarov.

215
00:17:45,862 --> 00:17:51,057
The two men who had each tried to save the other, together in the same bricks.

216
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:58,529
Meanwhile, in the same deadly years, one more American had died at the edge of space.

217
00:17:58,529 --> 00:18:03,276
Not in a capsule. In something far stranger. A rocket with wings.

218
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:07,561
Chapter seven. The plane that flew too high.

219
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:11,445
This is the X-15. Half aircraft, half rocket.

220
00:18:11,445 --> 00:18:18,653
Dropped from under the wing of a B-52, it could fly to the edge of space and land like a plane.

221
00:18:19,799 --> 00:18:25,998
It still holds the record for the fastest crewed aircraft flight in history. Mach 6.7.

222
00:18:25,998 --> 00:18:28,890
Almost seven times the speed of sound.

223
00:18:30,033 --> 00:18:37,054
Its pilots flew so high that several of them earned astronaut wings without ever riding a rocket from a launch pad.

224
00:18:37,054 --> 00:18:38,392
Among them, Michael Adams.

225
00:18:39,533 --> 00:18:45,996
November 15, 1967. Adams drops away from the B-52 and lights the engine.

226
00:18:45,996 --> 00:18:50,968
He climbs to eighty one kilometers. The sky turns black.

227
00:18:50,968 --> 00:18:54,449
He is, by American definition, in space.

228
00:18:55,566 --> 00:18:58,257
But something is wrong in the cockpit.

229
00:18:58,257 --> 00:19:01,716
An electrical disturbance is feeding his instruments bad information.

230
00:19:01,716 --> 00:19:06,328
And Adams is suffering from vertigo, a known issue on these flights.

231
00:19:07,466 --> 00:19:15,107
At the top of the arc, where the air is too thin for wings to matter, the plane slowly yaws sideways.

232
00:19:15,107 --> 00:19:17,654
Adams may believe he is only rolling.

233
00:19:18,799 --> 00:19:22,592
The X-15 reenters the atmosphere at Mach 5, flying sideways.

234
00:19:22,592 --> 00:19:28,281
It is like hitting a wall of air. The plane whips into a hypersonic spin.

235
00:19:29,399 --> 00:19:32,886
Incredibly, Adams fights it and recovers from the spin.

236
00:19:32,886 --> 00:19:40,632
But the recovery drops him into a dive, and the flight control system begins to oscillate, pitching harder and harder.

237
00:19:41,766 --> 00:19:45,440
The forces climb past anything the airframe can survive.

238
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:51,562
At eighteen kilometers, moving at nearly four thousand kilometers per hour, the X-15 breaks apart.

239
00:19:52,699 --> 00:19:56,718
Michael Adams is killed. He is awarded astronaut wings posthumously.

240
00:19:56,718 --> 00:20:03,148
The only fatality, in a hundred and ninety nine flights of the fastest aircraft ever built.

241
00:20:04,266 --> 00:20:07,192
The X-15 program quietly ended a year later.

242
00:20:07,192 --> 00:20:13,775
But its data shaped everything that came after, including a vehicle we will meet later in this story.

243
00:20:13,775 --> 00:20:14,872
The Space Shuttle.

244
00:20:15,999 --> 00:20:24,515
Four years after Adams fell, the Soviet Union achieved something magnificent, and then suffered the most haunting disaster in the history of spaceflight.

245
00:20:25,633 --> 00:20:29,421
Chapter eight. The only humans to die in space.

246
00:20:30,566 --> 00:20:35,050
June 1971. The Soviet Union has just done something extraordinary.

247
00:20:35,050 --> 00:20:41,328
Three cosmonauts are living aboard Salyut 1, the first space station in human history.

248
00:20:42,466 --> 00:20:46,637
Georgy Dobrovolsky, the commander. Vladislav Volkov, the flight engineer.

249
00:20:46,637 --> 00:20:48,491
Viktor Patsayev, the researcher.

250
00:20:48,491 --> 00:20:54,979
For twenty three days, they are the most famous men in the Soviet Union.

251
00:20:56,100 --> 00:20:59,951
Nightly television broadcasts show them floating, joking, working.

252
00:20:59,951 --> 00:21:06,209
A world record for the longest spaceflight. The mission is a total triumph.

253
00:21:07,333 --> 00:21:09,520
There is one detail that will matter.

254
00:21:09,520 --> 00:21:13,582
The Soyuz ferry is cramped, so the crew does not wear pressure suits.

255
00:21:13,582 --> 00:21:15,457
There is no room for them.

256
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:21,813
June 29th. The crew undocks and heads home. Everything is normal.

257
00:21:21,813 --> 00:21:26,553
The deorbit burn is perfect. The modules separate on schedule.

258
00:21:27,700 --> 00:21:32,855
Except the explosive bolts joining the modules fire all at once, instead of in sequence.

259
00:21:32,855 --> 00:21:38,698
The jolt shakes the whole ship. And deep in the hull, a small valve is knocked open.

260
00:21:39,833 --> 00:21:43,745
The valve is sixteen millimeters across. The size of a coin.

261
00:21:43,745 --> 00:21:49,080
It is meant to open near the ground, to equalize the cabin with fresh air.

262
00:21:50,200 --> 00:21:53,726
Instead it opens at one hundred and sixty eight kilometers.

263
00:21:53,726 --> 00:21:59,368
On the other side of that coin sized hole, there is nothing. The vacuum of space.

264
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:03,124
The cabin air screams out through the valve.

265
00:22:03,124 --> 00:22:06,405
The crew hears the hiss and feels the pressure dropping.

266
00:22:06,405 --> 00:22:08,701
They have seconds to find the leak.

267
00:22:09,833 --> 00:22:13,443
Patsayev locates it and starts cranking the manual valve shut.

268
00:22:13,443 --> 00:22:17,054
It is designed to take almost a minute to close.

269
00:22:17,054 --> 00:22:19,942
He has perhaps fifteen seconds of useful consciousness.

270
00:22:21,066 --> 00:22:24,500
The cabin pressure hits zero in under two minutes.

271
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:28,698
In vacuum, the gases in their blood bubble out of solution.

272
00:22:28,698 --> 00:22:31,750
All three men are gone, quickly and quietly.

273
00:22:32,899 --> 00:22:37,785
And here is the haunting part. The spacecraft does not care.

274
00:22:37,785 --> 00:22:41,337
The automatic systems fly the entire reentry perfectly.

275
00:22:42,466 --> 00:22:46,711
The parachute opens on time. The soft landing rockets fire.

276
00:22:46,711 --> 00:22:52,654
The capsule settles gently onto the Kazakh steppe, right on target. A flawless landing.

277
00:22:53,799 --> 00:22:58,710
Recovery helicopters land beside it. The crews knock on the hull, smiling.

278
00:22:58,710 --> 00:23:00,347
There is no answer.

279
00:23:00,347 --> 00:23:06,076
They open the hatch and find all three cosmonauts still strapped in their couches.

280
00:23:07,199 --> 00:23:10,987
They look asleep. There is not a mark on them.

281
00:23:10,987 --> 00:23:16,290
The rescuers attempt resuscitation for a long time. It is far, far too late.

282
00:23:17,433 --> 00:23:22,222
The autopsies find brain hemorrhages and nitrogen bubbles in their blood.

283
00:23:22,222 --> 00:23:27,882
The evidence of vacuum. Dobrovolsky, Volkov and Patsayev had been dead since orbit.

284
00:23:28,999 --> 00:23:35,138
They remain, to this day, the only human beings who have died in space itself.

285
00:23:35,138 --> 00:23:37,593
Above the line. In the black.

286
00:23:38,733 --> 00:23:44,022
The Soviet Union gave them a state funeral, and then grounded Soyuz for two years.

287
00:23:44,022 --> 00:23:48,607
When it flew again, every crew wore pressure suits for launch and landing.

288
00:23:49,733 --> 00:23:56,296
That rule has never been broken since. It is written, as engineers say, in blood.

289
00:23:56,296 --> 00:23:58,484
Every rule in spaceflight is.

290
00:23:59,633 --> 00:24:04,679
But the deadliest day in the history of the space race was not a flight at all.

291
00:24:04,679 --> 00:24:09,429
It happened on a launch pad, eleven years earlier, and it was hidden for three decades.

292
00:24:10,566 --> 00:24:13,727
Chapter nine. The disaster that never happened.

293
00:24:14,866 --> 00:24:17,585
October 1960. Baikonur, Soviet Kazakhstan.

294
00:24:17,585 --> 00:24:26,830
On the pad stands the R-16, a new intercontinental ballistic missile, fully loaded with toxic hypergolic fuel.

295
00:24:27,966 --> 00:24:30,778
Hypergolic propellants do not need a spark.

296
00:24:30,778 --> 00:24:34,794
The fuel and oxidizer explode on contact with each other.

297
00:24:34,794 --> 00:24:37,605
The engineers call the stuff devil's venom.

298
00:24:38,733 --> 00:24:43,996
The missile is riddled with electrical problems. The safe procedure is obvious.

299
00:24:43,996 --> 00:24:49,260
Drain the fuel, fix the faults, start again. That would take days.

300
00:24:50,399 --> 00:24:59,228
But Marshal Mitrofan Nedelin, commander of the strategic rocket forces, is under pressure from Moscow to deliver before the anniversary of the revolution.

301
00:25:00,366 --> 00:25:04,546
So the order is given. Fix the missile on the pad. Fueled.

302
00:25:04,546 --> 00:25:10,815
And to show his confidence, Nedelin has a chair placed beside the rocket, and sits down to watch.

303
00:25:11,933 --> 00:25:15,550
Dozens of engineers and soldiers swarm the fully loaded missile.

304
00:25:15,550 --> 00:25:21,337
Safety protocols dissolve. At one point there are more than a hundred people on the pad.

305
00:25:22,466 --> 00:25:28,196
During testing, a sequencer in the second stage is left in the wrong position.

306
00:25:28,196 --> 00:25:32,288
When power is applied, the second stage engine simply... starts.

307
00:25:33,433 --> 00:25:38,608
Its exhaust torches straight down into the fully loaded first stage beneath it.

308
00:25:38,608 --> 00:25:43,386
The tanks rupture. Hundreds of tons of devil's venom meet each other.

309
00:25:44,533 --> 00:25:50,044
The fireball consumes the pad in seconds. It is over three thousand degrees.

310
00:25:50,044 --> 00:25:53,859
People closest to the rocket simply cease to exist.

311
00:25:54,999 --> 00:26:02,557
Those further away run, but the burning fuel spreads faster than a man can sprint, and the security fence traps them.

312
00:26:02,557 --> 00:26:04,716
Automatic cameras film all of it.

313
00:26:05,866 --> 00:26:10,181
The officially acknowledged death toll, decades later, was seventy eight.

314
00:26:10,181 --> 00:26:14,065
Other credible estimates run past one hundred and twenty.

315
00:26:14,065 --> 00:26:17,517
Among the dead, Marshal Nedelin, in his chair.

316
00:26:18,666 --> 00:26:21,644
It remains the deadliest launch pad disaster in history.

317
00:26:21,644 --> 00:26:24,622
And the Soviet response was to make it disappear.

318
00:26:25,766 --> 00:26:31,463
The dead were buried quietly, some in a mass grave. Families were told of accidents.

319
00:26:31,463 --> 00:26:35,640
Pravda announced that Marshal Nedelin had died in a plane crash.

320
00:26:36,766 --> 00:26:40,500
For twenty nine years, the disaster officially never happened.

321
00:26:40,500 --> 00:26:47,137
The Soviet Union only admitted the truth in 1989, when a magazine finally published the story.

322
00:26:48,266 --> 00:26:50,006
Think about what that means.

323
00:26:50,006 --> 00:26:57,663
The worst disaster of the space age was erased so completely that the world mourned a man who died in a fireball...

324
00:26:57,663 --> 00:26:59,055
for a plane crash.

325
00:27:00,199 --> 00:27:02,356
And Nedelin was not the last.

326
00:27:02,356 --> 00:27:09,185
Rockets kept killing people on the ground, all over the world, in disasters most people have never heard of.

327
00:27:10,333 --> 00:27:13,494
Chapter ten. The disasters you've never heard of.

328
00:27:14,633 --> 00:27:18,587
March 1980. Plesetsk cosmodrome, in the forests of northern Russia.

329
00:27:18,587 --> 00:27:22,937
A Vostok rocket is being fueled for a routine satellite launch.

330
00:27:22,937 --> 00:27:26,101
Dozens of soldiers are working on the pad.

331
00:27:27,233 --> 00:27:31,714
During fueling, the rocket explodes. Three hundred tons of propellant.

332
00:27:31,714 --> 00:27:38,884
Forty four men die on the pad, four more later of their burns. Forty eight dead.

333
00:27:40,033 --> 00:27:45,145
The Soviet Union does exactly what it did after Nedelin. Silence.

334
00:27:45,145 --> 00:27:51,187
The disaster stays secret for nine years. The investigation quietly blames the soldiers.

335
00:27:52,333 --> 00:27:56,530
Only in the 1990s does a new investigation clear the dead.

336
00:27:56,530 --> 00:28:00,727
The real cause was a manufacturing change in the fuel filters.

337
00:28:00,727 --> 00:28:03,017
The men were innocent all along.

338
00:28:04,166 --> 00:28:06,310
February 1996. Xichang, China.

339
00:28:06,310 --> 00:28:15,959
A Long March 3B rocket, the most powerful China has ever built, lifts off carrying an American-built satellite.

340
00:28:15,959 --> 00:28:17,567
Cameras are rolling.

341
00:28:18,699 --> 00:28:21,918
Two seconds after liftoff, the rocket tips sideways.

342
00:28:21,918 --> 00:28:26,343
It flies almost horizontally for twenty two seconds, clears the pad...

343
00:28:26,343 --> 00:28:30,768
and dives into the hillside village next to the launch center.

344
00:28:31,899 --> 00:28:37,287
The explosion levels dozens of buildings. The official toll is six dead.

345
00:28:37,287 --> 00:28:43,573
Western witnesses who saw the aftermath believe the real number may be far higher.

346
00:28:43,573 --> 00:28:46,266
It has never been independently verified.

347
00:28:47,399 --> 00:28:51,615
Foreign guests were kept in a bunker, then driven out past the ruins.

348
00:28:51,615 --> 00:28:56,803
The footage that leaked out is still some of the most chilling rocket video ever recorded.

349
00:28:57,933 --> 00:28:59,705
August 2003. Alcântara, Brazil.

350
00:28:59,705 --> 00:29:08,121
The country is days from its dream of becoming a space power, with its own satellite launcher, the VLS-1.

351
00:29:09,266 --> 00:29:14,987
With a hundred people working on and around the pad, one of the four boosters ignites by itself.

352
00:29:14,987 --> 00:29:16,894
There is no warning at all.

353
00:29:18,033 --> 00:29:21,398
The rocket and tower are destroyed in seconds.

354
00:29:21,398 --> 00:29:26,025
Twenty one people die, most of them senior engineers and technicians.

355
00:29:26,025 --> 00:29:30,232
A generation of Brazilian rocket expertise, gone in one afternoon.

356
00:29:31,366 --> 00:29:33,631
Brazil's satellite launcher program never recovered.

357
00:29:33,631 --> 00:29:38,915
To this day, the country has not orbited a satellite on its own rocket.

358
00:29:40,033 --> 00:29:41,263
Add them up.

359
00:29:41,263 --> 00:29:46,186
The people who died making spaceflight possible without ever wearing a spacesuit.

360
00:29:46,186 --> 00:29:51,109
They outnumber the astronauts many times over. History barely remembers their names.

361
00:29:52,233 --> 00:29:56,421
But there is one group of space deaths stranger than all of these.

362
00:29:56,421 --> 00:29:58,999
Deaths that may never have happened at all.

363
00:30:00,133 --> 00:30:02,824
Chapter eleven. The lost cosmonauts.

364
00:30:03,966 --> 00:30:06,669
Turin, Italy. The late 1950s.

365
00:30:06,669 --> 00:30:16,400
Two teenage brothers, Achille and Giovanni Judica-Cordiglia, build a homemade radio listening station in an old German bunker.

366
00:30:17,533 --> 00:30:19,730
They are good. Astonishingly good.

367
00:30:19,730 --> 00:30:27,642
They intercept Sputnik's beeps, the heartbeat of the dog Laika, signals the Soviets never meant anyone to hear.

368
00:30:28,766 --> 00:30:35,079
And then, they claim, they start hearing something else. Human voices. In Russian.

369
00:30:35,079 --> 00:30:39,450
From space. On missions that officially do not exist.

370
00:30:40,599 --> 00:30:46,033
A garbled S O S, fading away as if drifting into deep space.

371
00:30:46,033 --> 00:30:52,720
Labored breathing and a failing heartbeat. And in May 1961, the one that made them famous.

372
00:30:53,866 --> 00:31:01,518
A woman's voice, distressed, saying in Russian that she can see flames, asking, am I going to crash?

373
00:31:01,518 --> 00:31:07,894
Then silence. If real, a female cosmonaut died two years before the first one flew.

374
00:31:09,033 --> 00:31:11,459
The theory spread across the world.

375
00:31:11,459 --> 00:31:15,907
That Gagarin was not the first, just the first to survive.

376
00:31:15,907 --> 00:31:19,142
That the Soviet Union simply erased the failures.

377
00:31:20,266 --> 00:31:23,961
And you can see why people believed it.

378
00:31:23,961 --> 00:31:30,888
This very video is full of things the Soviet Union really did erase. Bondarenko. Nedelin.

379
00:31:30,888 --> 00:31:33,197
Plesetsk. The pattern was real.

380
00:31:34,333 --> 00:31:37,582
But here is what the evidence actually says.

381
00:31:37,582 --> 00:31:42,457
When historians and audio analysts examined the recordings, the details fall apart.

382
00:31:43,599 --> 00:31:48,608
The Russian in the tapes contains errors no trained Soviet pilot would make.

383
00:31:48,608 --> 00:31:52,846
Cosmonauts followed strict radio procedure. The transmissions follow none of it.

384
00:31:53,966 --> 00:31:55,895
The orbits do not work either.

385
00:31:55,895 --> 00:32:01,359
Several signals could not physically have come from a spacecraft passing over Italy at the claimed times.

386
00:32:02,499 --> 00:32:12,544
And when Soviet archives finally opened in the 1990s, researchers who documented every real cover-up in this video found no trace of any dead cosmonaut before Gagarin.

387
00:32:12,544 --> 00:32:14,776
The secret rosters are accounted for.

388
00:32:15,899 --> 00:32:18,817
The lost cosmonauts are almost certainly a myth.

389
00:32:18,817 --> 00:32:22,099
But the myth survived for one very good reason.

390
00:32:22,099 --> 00:32:25,381
The truth beside it was worse than the fiction.

391
00:32:26,499 --> 00:32:28,753
You did not need phantom cosmonauts.

392
00:32:28,753 --> 00:32:34,388
The real ones, the ones with names and graves, were being erased in broad daylight.

393
00:32:35,533 --> 00:32:40,804
Now we come to the two disasters everyone remembers. Because these were not hidden.

394
00:32:40,804 --> 00:32:46,452
They happened on live television, in front of the whole world. And children were watching.

395
00:32:47,599 --> 00:32:50,681
Chapter twelve. Seventy three seconds.

396
00:32:51,799 --> 00:32:57,794
January 28, 1986. Cape Canaveral is freezing. Icicles hang from the launch tower.

397
00:32:57,794 --> 00:33:02,405
The Space Shuttle Challenger stands ready for its tenth flight.

398
00:33:03,533 --> 00:33:05,310
On board are seven crew.

399
00:33:05,310 --> 00:33:13,486
Among them, Christa McAuliffe, a high school teacher from New Hampshire, chosen from eleven thousand applicants to be the first teacher in space.

400
00:33:14,633 --> 00:33:18,211
Because of her, classrooms across America are watching live.

401
00:33:18,211 --> 00:33:21,790
Millions of children, waiting for their teacher to fly.

402
00:33:22,933 --> 00:33:30,247
What the children do not know is that the night before, engineers at the booster manufacturer begged NASA not to launch.

403
00:33:31,366 --> 00:33:35,445
The shuttle's solid rocket boosters are sealed with rubber O-rings.

404
00:33:35,445 --> 00:33:41,972
In cold weather, the rubber stiffens and seals slowly. It has never been tested this cold.

405
00:33:43,099 --> 00:33:47,627
Engineer Bob Ebeling tells his wife that night, it's going to blow up.

406
00:33:47,627 --> 00:33:52,503
His colleague Roger Boisjoly has been warning about the O-rings for months, in writing.

407
00:33:53,633 --> 00:33:57,757
Managers overrule them. The launch has already been delayed.

408
00:33:57,757 --> 00:34:00,507
The pressure to fly is enormous.

409
00:34:00,507 --> 00:34:04,631
The overnight temperature falls far below any previous launch.

410
00:34:05,766 --> 00:34:10,132
At liftoff, a camera catches what no one sees in real time.

411
00:34:10,132 --> 00:34:13,771
Puffs of black smoke from the right booster's lowest joint.

412
00:34:13,771 --> 00:34:15,954
The O-rings have failed at ignition.

413
00:34:17,099 --> 00:34:21,814
By pure chance, molten fuel byproduct temporarily plugs the leak.

414
00:34:21,814 --> 00:34:27,470
For almost a minute, Challenger flies normally. The crew feels nothing wrong.

415
00:34:28,599 --> 00:34:34,119
Then Challenger flies into the strongest wind shear ever recorded on a shuttle flight.

416
00:34:34,119 --> 00:34:37,272
The stack flexes. The fragile plug shatters loose.

417
00:34:38,399 --> 00:34:44,590
A jet of flame cuts sideways out of the booster like a blowtorch, straight onto the giant external fuel tank.

418
00:34:45,733 --> 00:34:55,450
Seventy three seconds after liftoff, the tank collapses and the vehicle is torn apart at twice the speed of sound, in a fireball broadcast into classrooms across the country.

419
00:34:56,599 --> 00:35:01,510
The last words on the crew recorder come from pilot Michael Smith. Uh oh.

420
00:35:02,633 --> 00:35:07,662
Here is what most people do not know. Challenger did not explode like a bomb.

421
00:35:07,662 --> 00:35:10,679
The crew cabin tore free of the fireball, intact.

422
00:35:11,799 --> 00:35:18,225
It continued upward for another five kilometers before arcing over into a two and a half minute fall to the ocean.

423
00:35:19,366 --> 00:35:25,361
Investigators later found that at least some of the crew's emergency air packs had been manually activated.

424
00:35:25,361 --> 00:35:29,240
Somebody was alive, and working the problem, on the way down.

425
00:35:30,366 --> 00:35:34,482
The cabin hit the Atlantic at over three hundred kilometers per hour.

426
00:35:34,482 --> 00:35:38,255
No one could have survived the impact. All seven were lost.

427
00:35:39,399 --> 00:35:43,930
The investigation that followed exposed everything. The years of O-ring warnings.

428
00:35:43,930 --> 00:35:44,754
The memos.

429
00:35:44,754 --> 00:35:51,755
The meeting where engineers were told to take off their engineering hats and put on management hats.

430
00:35:52,899 --> 00:36:02,068
Physicist Richard Feynman ended the argument on live television, by dropping a piece of O-ring rubber into a glass of ice water and showing it lost its resilience.

431
00:36:03,199 --> 00:36:08,169
His written conclusion is the most quoted sentence in engineering ethics.

432
00:36:08,169 --> 00:36:15,398
For a successful technology, reality must take precedence over public relations, for nature cannot be fooled.

433
00:36:16,533 --> 00:36:22,811
The shuttle was grounded for almost three years. NASA promised the culture had changed.

434
00:36:22,811 --> 00:36:27,295
Seventeen years later, the same culture killed seven more people.

435
00:36:28,433 --> 00:36:32,064
Chapter thirteen. Sixteen days, one hole.

436
00:36:33,200 --> 00:36:34,916
January 16, 2003.

437
00:36:34,916 --> 00:36:44,646
Space Shuttle Columbia, the oldest orbiter in the fleet, lifts off on a sixteen day science mission.

438
00:36:44,646 --> 00:36:45,791
Flight STS-107.

439
00:36:46,933 --> 00:36:55,762
Eighty two seconds in, a piece of insulating foam the size of a briefcase breaks off the external tank and strikes the left wing at hundreds of kilometers per hour.

440
00:36:56,900 --> 00:37:01,749
Foam had been falling off tanks for years. It had never destroyed a shuttle.

441
00:37:01,749 --> 00:37:06,251
So it had been reclassified from a safety hazard... to a maintenance issue.

442
00:37:07,400 --> 00:37:12,034
That is a pattern with a name. Normalization of deviance.

443
00:37:12,034 --> 00:37:18,059
When you get away with something dangerous long enough, it stops feeling dangerous.

444
00:37:18,059 --> 00:37:19,913
Challenger, all over again.

445
00:37:21,033 --> 00:37:27,575
Engineers who reviewed the launch film were alarmed and requested spy satellite images of the wing.

446
00:37:27,575 --> 00:37:30,437
Managers turned the requests down. Three times.

447
00:37:31,566 --> 00:37:38,129
The crew was told about the strike, in an email that reassured them it was nothing to worry about.

448
00:37:38,129 --> 00:37:40,892
There is, quote, absolutely no concern for entry.

449
00:37:42,033 --> 00:37:46,155
In truth, the foam had punched a hole in the wing's carbon leading edge.

450
00:37:46,155 --> 00:37:51,750
A hole the size of a dinner plate, in the one place that takes the most heat coming home.

451
00:37:52,900 --> 00:37:58,527
For sixteen days, Columbia orbited with a mortal wound no one could see.

452
00:37:58,527 --> 00:38:02,423
The crew ran their experiments. They called their families.

453
00:38:02,423 --> 00:38:05,020
They took photographs of the Earth.

454
00:38:06,166 --> 00:38:11,293
February 1st. Columbia begins reentry over the Pacific, heading for Florida.

455
00:38:11,293 --> 00:38:17,817
Plasma at over fifteen hundred degrees streams over the wings. And into the hole.

456
00:38:18,966 --> 00:38:25,413
Inside the left wing, the superheated gas melts the aluminum skeleton, strut by strut.

457
00:38:25,413 --> 00:38:31,400
Sensors begin dropping offline, one by one. In mission control, a controller notices.

458
00:38:32,533 --> 00:38:35,242
Over Texas, at Mach 18, the wing fails.

459
00:38:35,242 --> 00:38:42,015
Columbia tumbles and breaks apart in a shower of fire that thousands of people see and film from the ground.

460
00:38:43,133 --> 00:38:49,507
The last transmission from commander Rick Husband is cut off mid word. Roger, uh...

461
00:38:50,633 --> 00:38:58,500
All seven crew are lost. Rick Husband. Willie McCool. Michael Anderson. Kalpana Chawla.

462
00:38:58,500 --> 00:39:05,157
David Brown. Laurel Clark. And Ilan Ramon, the first Israeli astronaut.

463
00:39:06,300 --> 00:39:11,860
Kalpana Chawla had grown up in a small town in Haryana, India, dreaming of flight.

464
00:39:11,860 --> 00:39:15,939
She remains a national hero to hundreds of millions of people.

465
00:39:17,066 --> 00:39:21,110
Debris rained over two thousand locations across Texas and Louisiana.

466
00:39:21,110 --> 00:39:27,175
Twenty five thousand people walked the fields for months, recovering the shuttle piece by piece.

467
00:39:28,300 --> 00:39:31,088
The investigation board did not mince words.

468
00:39:31,088 --> 00:39:37,063
NASA's culture, it wrote, had as much to do with this accident as the foam.

469
00:39:37,063 --> 00:39:39,454
The echo of 1986 was deafening.

470
00:39:40,600 --> 00:39:44,374
The most chilling part of the report is a study NASA ran afterward.

471
00:39:44,374 --> 00:39:48,149
Could the crew have been saved, if everyone had acted on day two?

472
00:39:49,266 --> 00:39:51,596
The answer was a desperate maybe.

473
00:39:51,596 --> 00:39:56,257
Shuttle Atlantis could conceivably have been rushed to launch for a rescue.

474
00:39:56,257 --> 00:40:00,917
It would have been the riskiest mission ever flown. But it existed.

475
00:40:02,066 --> 00:40:08,192
Nobody tried, because nobody looked, because nobody in charge believed there was a problem.

476
00:40:08,192 --> 00:40:10,817
Seven people died of institutional certainty.

477
00:40:11,966 --> 00:40:13,903
Columbia ended the shuttle era.

478
00:40:13,903 --> 00:40:18,938
The fleet flew again to finish the space station, then retired in 2011.

479
00:40:18,938 --> 00:40:23,199
Fourteen of the seventeen American spaceflight deaths happened on the shuttle.

480
00:40:24,333 --> 00:40:29,758
Those are the catastrophes. But space has also come within seconds of killing many more.

481
00:40:29,758 --> 00:40:33,737
And the survival stories are almost as terrifying as the losses.

482
00:40:34,866 --> 00:40:37,792
Chapter fourteen. The near misses.

483
00:40:38,933 --> 00:40:39,729
March 1965.

484
00:40:39,729 --> 00:40:46,099
Alexei Leonov floats out of Voskhod 2 and becomes the first human to walk in space.

485
00:40:46,099 --> 00:40:50,479
Twelve minutes of glory. Then he tries to come back inside.

486
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:54,985
In vacuum, his suit has ballooned like a football.

487
00:40:54,985 --> 00:40:57,994
His fingers have pulled out of his gloves.

488
00:40:57,994 --> 00:41:01,004
He physically cannot fit back through the airlock.

489
00:41:02,133 --> 00:41:03,672
Leonov does the unthinkable.

490
00:41:03,672 --> 00:41:12,139
He opens a valve and bleeds air out of his own suit, risking the bends, until it deflates enough to squeeze inside.

491
00:41:12,139 --> 00:41:13,679
He barely makes it.

492
00:41:14,800 --> 00:41:24,596
Then the capsule's automatic reentry fails, the crew lands four hundred kilometers off course in the Ural forest, and spends two nights in deep snow surrounded by wolves and bears.

493
00:41:25,733 --> 00:41:28,034
One year later, March 1966.

494
00:41:28,034 --> 00:41:34,016
Gemini 8, with Neil Armstrong in command, docks with an uncrewed target vehicle.

495
00:41:34,016 --> 00:41:37,697
Minutes later, the joined spacecraft begin to spin.

496
00:41:38,833 --> 00:41:41,164
A thruster is stuck firing.

497
00:41:41,164 --> 00:41:46,292
After undocking, the spin accelerates to one full revolution per second.

498
00:41:46,292 --> 00:41:50,954
The astronauts' vision starts to blur. Blackout is seconds away.

499
00:41:52,100 --> 00:41:58,745
On the edge of consciousness, Armstrong kills the main thrusters and regains control using the reentry system alone.

500
00:41:58,745 --> 00:42:03,176
Three years later, the same calm would land him on the Moon.

501
00:42:04,300 --> 00:42:10,151
April 1970. Apollo 13, two hundred and five thousand miles from Earth.

502
00:42:10,151 --> 00:42:17,465
A damaged oxygen tank explodes, and the words become legend. Houston, we've had a problem.

503
00:42:18,600 --> 00:42:21,113
The explosion guts the service module.

504
00:42:21,113 --> 00:42:25,302
Oxygen venting into space, power dying, the Moon landing gone.

505
00:42:25,302 --> 00:42:30,329
Three men, in a dying ship, a quarter million miles from home.

506
00:42:31,466 --> 00:42:39,747
They survive by moving into the lunar lander and using it as a lifeboat, looping around the Moon in freezing darkness while the world holds its breath.

507
00:42:40,866 --> 00:42:50,505
When the carbon dioxide starts to poison them, mission control invents a filter adapter out of plastic bags, cardboard and tape, and reads the instructions up by voice.

508
00:42:51,633 --> 00:42:57,557
Apollo 13 splashes down safely with all three crew. NASA calls it the successful failure.

509
00:42:57,557 --> 00:43:01,507
It remains the greatest rescue in the history of spaceflight.

510
00:43:02,633 --> 00:43:09,709
And if you think the danger belongs to history, meet Luca Parmitano. July 2013.

511
00:43:09,709 --> 00:43:12,742
International Space Station. A routine spacewalk.

512
00:43:13,866 --> 00:43:17,978
Forty four minutes in, water begins pooling inside his helmet.

513
00:43:17,978 --> 00:43:22,913
A failed pump is pushing his cooling water into his air supply.

514
00:43:22,913 --> 00:43:26,614
In zero gravity, water does not drip. It clings.

515
00:43:27,733 --> 00:43:33,431
The blob crawls over the back of his head, into his ears, then over his eyes.

516
00:43:33,431 --> 00:43:38,417
Then it starts creeping toward his nose. He is beginning to drown. In space.

517
00:43:39,566 --> 00:43:46,698
Blinded, with his radio garbling, Parmitano navigates back to the airlock from memory, by feeling the safety cable with his gloves.

518
00:43:46,698 --> 00:43:51,112
He makes it with more than a liter of water in his helmet.

519
00:43:52,233 --> 00:43:57,839
Every one of these people came within seconds or centimeters of joining this video's list.

520
00:43:57,839 --> 00:44:01,950
The line between legend and memorial, in space, is that thin.

521
00:44:03,100 --> 00:44:05,869
Chapter fifteen. The new age.

522
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:10,983
You might think this list belongs to the Cold War.

523
00:44:10,983 --> 00:44:14,967
To capsules and shuttles and dead empires. It does not.

524
00:44:14,967 --> 00:44:18,154
The most recent chapter was written in 2014.

525
00:44:19,300 --> 00:44:23,980
Mojave desert, California. SpaceShipTwo, the VSS Enterprise, is the future.

526
00:44:23,980 --> 00:44:30,533
A privately built rocket plane designed to carry tourists to the edge of space.

527
00:44:31,666 --> 00:44:35,601
In the cockpit, two test pilots. Peter Siebold in command.

528
00:44:35,601 --> 00:44:41,109
Michael Alsbury, flying as co-pilot, one of the most experienced engineers on the program.

529
00:44:41,109 --> 00:44:44,257
It is his ninth flight on the ship.

530
00:44:45,400 --> 00:44:51,225
The carrier aircraft releases them at fourteen kilometers. The rocket motor lights.

531
00:44:51,225 --> 00:44:57,051
SpaceShipTwo accelerates through the speed of sound, climbing hard. Everything is nominal.

532
00:44:58,200 --> 00:45:01,520
SpaceShipTwo has a unique reentry system called the feather.

533
00:45:01,520 --> 00:45:05,948
The twin tails fold upward to slow the ship, like a shuttlecock.

534
00:45:05,948 --> 00:45:10,007
It is only ever supposed to unlock high above the atmosphere.

535
00:45:11,133 --> 00:45:17,402
Eleven seconds after ignition, at Mach zero point nine two, Alsbury unlocks the feather early.

536
00:45:17,402 --> 00:45:23,254
The checklist says Mach one point four. No one will ever know exactly why.

537
00:45:24,400 --> 00:45:30,886
At that speed, in thick air, the aerodynamic loads slam the unlocked tails upward on their own.

538
00:45:30,886 --> 00:45:36,991
No one commands it. The ship pitches violently and tears itself apart in about a second.

539
00:45:38,133 --> 00:45:40,140
Michael Alsbury is killed instantly.

540
00:45:40,140 --> 00:45:44,557
He is the first person to die on a commercial spaceflight.

541
00:45:44,557 --> 00:45:48,974
He was thirty nine, a husband, and a father of two.

542
00:45:50,100 --> 00:45:54,282
And then, one of the strangest survival stories in aviation history.

543
00:45:54,282 --> 00:45:56,944
Peter Siebold wakes up in the sky.

544
00:45:58,066 --> 00:46:06,528
The breakup has thrown him clear, still strapped to his seat, unconscious, at over fifteen kilometers, where there is barely any oxygen.

545
00:46:06,528 --> 00:46:09,220
He is not wearing a pressure suit.

546
00:46:10,366 --> 00:46:17,489
Somewhere in the fall he comes to, unbuckles from the seat, and his parachute deploys automatically.

547
00:46:17,489 --> 00:46:21,050
He hits the desert alive. Broken, but alive.

548
00:46:22,200 --> 00:46:26,706
The investigation found the deeper cause was not one pilot's hand.

549
00:46:26,706 --> 00:46:32,442
The system had no guard against a single human error made under crushing workload.

550
00:46:32,442 --> 00:46:37,769
One switch, thrown four seconds early, could destroy the ship. So it did.

551
00:46:38,900 --> 00:46:44,217
Sound familiar? A known weakness. A schedule. A single point of failure.

552
00:46:44,217 --> 00:46:50,864
The new space age inherited the old one's disease, on its very first fatal flight.

553
00:46:52,000 --> 00:47:01,091
As private crews multiply and moon programs return, the lesson of this entire hour is about to be tested again, at a scale spaceflight has never seen.

554
00:47:02,233 --> 00:47:05,159
Chapter sixteen. What the photographs mean.

555
00:47:06,300 --> 00:47:11,332
Twenty one people killed flying missions. Hundreds more on launch pads and in training.

556
00:47:11,332 --> 00:47:15,286
Every one of them knew the risk, and strapped in anyway.

557
00:47:16,433 --> 00:47:22,341
And here is the pattern this hour has shown you. Almost none of them were killed by space.

558
00:47:22,341 --> 00:47:23,982
They were killed by decisions.

559
00:47:25,100 --> 00:47:30,194
A hatch that opened the wrong way. A launch approved against the engineers.

560
00:47:30,194 --> 00:47:35,288
A warning reclassified as paperwork. A schedule that mattered more than a life.

561
00:47:36,433 --> 00:47:40,800
The vacuum is not malicious. It is just utterly indifferent.

562
00:47:40,800 --> 00:47:47,352
As Feynman said, nature cannot be fooled. It only forgives you when you respect it.

563
00:47:48,500 --> 00:47:55,584
Every rule that keeps today's astronauts alive is written in the blood of the people in these photographs.

564
00:47:55,584 --> 00:48:00,307
The suits. The escape systems. The launch abort towers. All of it.

565
00:48:01,433 --> 00:48:05,978
Since Columbia, no astronaut has been lost on a spaceflight.

566
00:48:05,978 --> 00:48:10,524
Twenty three years and counting. That streak is their monument.

567
00:48:11,666 --> 00:48:15,441
Look one last time at Bruce McCandless, alone in the black.

568
00:48:15,441 --> 00:48:17,501
The most terrifying photo ever taken.

569
00:48:17,501 --> 00:48:21,619
Now you know why it is really just the most honest one.

570
00:48:22,766 --> 00:48:25,632
It shows exactly where we choose to go.

571
00:48:25,632 --> 00:48:30,289
A place with no air, no warmth, no mercy, and no second chances.

572
00:48:30,289 --> 00:48:32,797
And it shows that we go anyway.

573
00:48:33,933 --> 00:48:37,696
Because the people in this video did not die telling us to stop.

574
00:48:37,696 --> 00:48:41,169
Every one of them, to the last, was trying to go further.

575
00:48:42,300 --> 00:48:46,035
If this story moved you, the next one is already waiting.

576
00:48:46,035 --> 00:48:48,412
The scariest places in the known universe.

577
00:48:48,412 --> 00:48:52,827
And if you want more stories like this every week, subscribe to mindwired.

